etch from the public internet, or from reaching a service that only accepts connections from your server's network, at a public address. The protection that matters then is outside of chop. If you use `*`:"],[1040,"`CHOP_ALLOWED_HOSTS=*` makes chop an open image proxy: anyone who can send a request to chop can make it fetch and process images from any public host. The address checks, https, the size and time limits and the fetch limit still apply, but they can't stop someone from using your server and your bandwidth to fetch from the public internet, or from reaching a service that only accepts connections from your server's network, at a public address. The protection that matters then is outside of chop. If you use `*`:"],[79,"`CHOP_ALLOWED_INPUT_FORMATS=jpeg,png,webp` is for a site that only serves those. The format is detected from the content of the file, never the extension, and `jpg` is accepted as an alias of `jpeg`. A source in another format is a `415` with the message of any unsupported file, which doesn't tell which formats are allowed. It's rejected after the header is read and before any pixel is decoded or processed."],[778,"`CHOP_ALLOWED_INPUT_FORMATS=jpeg,png,webp` is for a site that only serves those. The format is detected from the content of the file, never the extension, and `jpg` is accepted as an alias of `jpeg`. A source in another format is a `415` with the message of any unsupported file, which doesn't tell which formats are allowed. It's rejected after the header is read and before any pixel is decoded or processed."],[78,"`CHOP_AUTO_FORMATS=webp` never negotiates AVIF, which avoids its encode cost. `CHOP_AUTO_FORMATS=none` disables all upgrades, so `auto` always gives the fallback format. `CHOP_AVIF=false` is a shorthand that removes `avif` from the list, also when `autoFormats` lists it. An explicit `format=avif` still works with either."],[777,"`CHOP_AUTO_FORMATS=webp` never negotiates AVIF, which avoids its encode cost. `CHOP_AUTO_FORMATS=none` disables all upgrades, so `auto` always gives the fallback format. `CHOP_AVIF=false` is a shorthand that removes `avif` from the list, also when `autoFormats` lists it. An explicit `format=avif` still works with either."],[380,"`CHOP_ORIGIN_ALLOW_PRIVATE=1` lets the configured origin be at a loopback, private or link-local address, like another container or a service on your private network. It applies only to the configured origin, never to a URL that a client supplied, and chop prints a warning on startup. Without it, an origin whose address is an IP literal in a blocked range is an invalid setting, and one whose name resolves to a blocked range is a `404`."],[1079,"`CHOP_ORIGIN_ALLOW_PRIVATE=1` lets the configured origin be at a loopback, private or link-local address, like another container or a service on your private network. It applies only to the configured origin, never to a URL that a client supplied, and chop prints a warning on startup. Without it, an origin whose address is an IP literal in a blocked range is an invalid setting, and one whose name resolves to a blocked range is a `404`."],[379,"`CHOP_ORIGIN_AUTH` is the value of an `Authorization` header, like `Bearer <token>`, for a private origin. It is only sent to the configured origin and never to URL sources, it is read from the environment only, and it is never logged. A value with a line break or a control character is an invalid setting."],[1078,"`CHOP_ORIGIN_AUTH` is the value of an `Authorization` header, like `Bearer <token>`, for a private origin. It is only sent to the configured origin and never to URL sources, it is read from the environment only, and it is never logged. A value with a line break or a control character is an invalid setting."],[318,"`CHOP_ORIGIN=s3://bucket` or `CHOP_ORIGIN=s3://bucket/prefix` reads the originals from a bucket, and `--origin s3://bucket` works too. The path of a request is appended to the prefix to make the key, so with `s3://my-bucket/uploads`