ms, translations or WordPress.org deploys, use the `wp-release` changeset commands instead. The comparison lists the differences."],[1130,"In a monorepo with 477 commits, `git subtree split` takes 6 to 8 seconds for one package and `subtree-split` takes 0.1 seconds. On WordPress/gutenberg (43,568 commits) it splits `packages/components` in 1.7 seconds. The hashes are the same as `git subtree split` makes, so a repository that you already split that way keeps its history when you switch."],[1183,"In a monorepo with 477 commits, `git subtree split` takes 6 to 8 seconds for one package and `subtree-split` takes 0.1 seconds. On WordPress/gutenberg (43,568 commits) it splits `packages/components` in 1.7 seconds. The hashes are the same as `git subtree split` makes, so a repository that you already split that way keeps its history when you switch."],[1236,"In a monorepo with 477 commits, `git subtree split` takes 6 to 8 seconds for one package and `subtree-split` takes 0.1 seconds. On WordPress/gutenberg (43,568 commits) it splits `packages/components` in 1.7 seconds. The hashes are the same as `git subtree split` makes, so a repository that you already split that way keeps its history when you switch."],[4696,"In CI, `changeset status --since origin/main` (or `changeset status` with a configured `baseBranch` of `main`) requires a changeset added by the pull request for every package that it changes. Changesets already on the base don't count, and an empty changeset added by the pull request records an explicit no-release decision. A package at the repository root owns every changed file. The command exits with code 2 and reports `Changed package \"…\" has no changeset.` for each missing one; with the `github` log format, the error is an annotation on a changed file of the package. `--since` needs the history of the base, so check out with `fetch-depth: 0`."],[4882,"In CI, `changeset status --since origin/main` (or `changeset status` with a configured `baseBranch` of `mai