he repository, so wp-release assumes no directory structure or naming convention of its own:"],[2148,"The config describes the repository, so wp-release assumes no directory structure or naming convention of its own:"],[2570,"The config describes the repository, so wp-release assumes no directory structure or naming convention of its own:"],[2992,"The config describes the repository, so wp-release assumes no directory structure or naming convention of its own:"],[3414,"The config describes the repository, so wp-release assumes no directory structure or naming convention of its own:"],[3836,"The config describes the repository, so wp-release assumes no directory structure or naming convention of its own:"],[4271,"The config describes the repository, so wp-release assumes no directory structure or naming convention of its own:"],[4760,"The config file is regular PHP (loaded with `require`) and must return an array, normally the result of `define_config()`, which validates the settings and hooks eagerly at load time and returns their canonical shape. The config helpers are namespaced functions, the PHP analogs of the `wp-release` `defineConfig()` and descriptor builders; import them with `use function`."],[4946,"The config file is regular PHP (loaded with `require`) and must return an array, normally the result of `define_config()`, which validates the settings and hooks eagerly at load time and returns their canonical shape. The config helpers are namespaced functions, the PHP analogs of the `wp-release` `defineConfig()` and descriptor builders; import them with `use function`."],[5132,"The config file is regular PHP (loaded with `require`) and must return an array, normally the result of `define_config()`, which validates the settings and hooks eagerly at load time and returns their canonical shape. The config helpers are namespaced functions, the PHP analogs of the `wp-release` `defineConfig()` and descriptor builders; import them with `use function`."],[4757,"The configuration lives in `changesets.config.php` at the project root, the analog of `wp-release.config.mjs`. The global `--config PATH` selects another file for one command."],[4943,"The configuration lives in `changesets.config.php` at the project root, the analog of `wp-release.config.mjs`. The global `--config PATH` selects another file for one command."],[5129,"The configuration lives in `changesets.config.php` at the project root, the analog of `wp-release.config.mjs`. The global `--config PATH` selects another file for one command."],[98,"The credentials of `CHOP_ORIGIN_AUTH` are never sent to a URL source, not even to the host of the configured origin. A URL source of that host is fetched like any other, with the address checks."],[797,"The credentials of `CHOP_ORIGIN_AUTH` are never sent to a URL source, not even to the host of the configured origin. A URL source of that host is fetched like any other, with the address checks."],[1568,"The default output is `<Domain Path>/<text-domain>.pot`, or `languages/<text-domain>.pot`. A leading `/` in `Domain Path` is package-relative, and configured destinations cannot escape the package. `--destination` changes the package-relative directory. An explicit `--output <file.pot>` resolves against the command's working directory and requires one package. All multi-package destinations are validated before writing; each completed package replaces its file atomically, independently of other packages. `--check` compares bytes without writing and fails if the POT is absent or stale. Source references are preserved by default as `file:line`. `--add-location file` keeps only the file name, so that editing a source file doesn't change the POT unless its strings change, and `--no-location` (the same as `--add-location never`) omits them; `--add-location full` is the default. In configuration, set `location` to `'full'`, `'file'` or `'never'`. The older `--no-references` flag and `references: false` option still work as `--no-locati