le sets how many there are. The monorepo check splits every package of this repository with both implementations. It takes a few minutes because of `git subtree split`, so it runs on demand."],[387,"The default scheme. The path is the path of the original and the arguments in Requests are in the query string. A path that starts with `http://` or `https://` (also `https:/` and `https%3A//`) is a URL source, like `/https://uploads.example.com/photo.jpg?w=300`, and the query string is still chop's arguments, so the source URL can't have its own."],[1086,"The default scheme. The path is the path of the original and the arguments in Requests are in the query string. A path that starts with `http://` or `https://` (also `https:/` and `https%3A//`) is a URL source, like `/https://uploads.example.com/photo.jpg?w=300`, and the query string is still chop's arguments, so the source URL can't have its own."],[1315,"The default strategy bumps stable `x.y.z` versions with SemVer rules, and the `x.y` and `x.y.z.w` versions common in WordPress plugins the same way: the bumped part is raised, the parts after it reset to `0`, and the version keeps its number of parts. A minor bump of `2.1` is `2.2` and a major bump is `3.0`, while a patch bump adds the third part, `2.1.1`. A patch bump of `1.2.3.4` is `1.2.4.0`, so raising only the fourth part needs a custom strategy. Configure `nextVersion( { package, bump, currentVersion, changesets } )` for other formats; changeset bumps remain `patch`, `minor` or `major`. Pre-releases are added to the next version with `--prerelease`, described below."],[1740,"The default strategy bumps stable `x.y.z` versions with SemVer rules, and the `x.y` and `x.y.z.w` versions common in WordPress plugins the same way: the bumped part is raised, the parts after it reset to `0`, and the version keeps its number of parts. A minor bump of `2.1` is `2.2` and a major bump is `3.0`, while a patch bump adds the third part, `2.1.1`. A patch bump of `1.2.3.4` is `1.2.